.TH LIBMU2EDAQ_DISCOVERY 3 "2026-09-30" "mu2edaq-discovery 1.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
libmu2edaq_discovery \- C++17 Responder for Mu2e DAQ service discovery
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
#include <mu2edaq_discovery/Responder.hpp>

namespace mu2edaq_discovery {
    constexpr const char* PROTO = "mu2edaq-discovery/1";
    constexpr const char* GROUP = "239.255.42.99";
    constexpr int PORT = 28999;
    constexpr int MAX_DATAGRAM = 1400;

    class Responder {
    public:
        struct Options {
            std::string name, app;
            int port = 0;
            std::string scheme, version = "0";
            std::map<std::string, std::string> meta;
            std::string host, group = GROUP;
            int listen_port = PORT;
            int announce_interval = 0;
            std::string bind_interface = "0.0.0.0";
        };
        explicit Responder(Options opts);
        ~Responder();
        void start();
        void stop();
        const std::string& instance_id() const;
    };
}
.EE
Link with
.BR mu2edaq_discovery::mu2edaq_discovery .
.SH DESCRIPTION
The C++ half of
.B mu2edaq\-discovery
is a responder only.
There is no C++ client; discovery scans are run from
.BR mu2edaq\-discover (1).
It exists so that C++ DAQ applications appear in the same scans as the Python
ones, and it speaks the identical wire protocol
.RB ( mu2edaq\-discovery (7)).
.PP
The library is dependency\-free: C++17 standard library plus POSIX sockets,
with a small internal JSON parser and serializer in
.IR Json.hpp .
Linux and macOS are supported; Windows would need a Winsock backend and does
not have one.
The only link dependency is
.BR Threads::Threads .
.PP
.B Responder
is neither copyable nor assignable.
The constructor fills defaults:
.I scheme
from
.IR app ,
.I host
from the resolved canonical FQDN, and
.I bind_interface
from
.BR 0.0.0.0 .
It generates the instance UUID and the
.B started
timestamp once, so both stay fixed for the lifetime of the object.
.SS start()
Creates the socket, sets
.B SO_REUSEADDR
and
.BR SO_REUSEPORT ,
binds
.I listen_port
on
.BR INADDR_ANY ,
joins the multicast group, sets a 0.5 s
.BR SO_RCVTIMEO ,
and then spawns the responder thread.
All of that happens on the
.I calling
thread, so a bind or membership failure throws
.B std::system_error
to the caller before any thread exists.
This differs from the Python
.BR Responder ,
whose
.B start()
cannot report such a failure.
.SS stop()
Sets the atomic stop flag, sends a zero\-length datagram to
.BI 127.0.0.1: listen_port
to break the blocking
.BR recvfrom (2),
joins the thread and closes the socket.
Safe to call more than once, and called from the destructor.
.SS Behaviour on the wire
The responder answers a DISCOVER whose
.B proto
and
.B type
match, applying
.I filter
keys
.BR app ", " name " and " host
with
.BR fnmatch (3).
A reply is delayed by a uniform random 0 to 250 ms and is sent unicast to the
datagram's source.
Malformed datagrams are caught and ignored.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I cpp/include/mu2edaq_discovery/Responder.hpp
Public API.
.TP
.I cpp/include/mu2edaq_discovery/Json.hpp
Header\-only minimal JSON.
.TP
.I cpp/src/Responder.cpp
Implementation.
.TP
.I cpp/CMakeLists.txt
Builds the static library, the demo and the ctest suite.
.TP
.I cpp/examples/responder_demo.cpp
A standalone responder for interop testing against the Python client.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
None.
All configuration is through
.BR Responder::Options .
.SH EXAMPLES
Consume via CMake
.BR FetchContent ,
as
.B mu2edaq\-trigger\-scalers
does:
.EX
    include(FetchContent)
    FetchContent_Declare(mu2edaq_discovery
        GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/Mu2e/mu2edaq-discovery.git
        GIT_TAG main
        SOURCE_SUBDIR cpp)
    FetchContent_MakeAvailable(mu2edaq_discovery)
    target_link_libraries(your_app PRIVATE
        mu2edaq_discovery::mu2edaq_discovery)
.EE
Advertise a service, tolerating a discovery failure:
.EX
    mu2edaq_discovery::Responder::Options opt;
    opt.name   = "Trigger Scalers";
    opt.app    = "trigger-scalers";
    opt.port   = 5557;
    opt.scheme = "udp";
    mu2edaq_discovery::Responder responder(opt);
    try {
        responder.start();
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cerr << "[Discovery] not started: " << e.what() << "\\n";
    }
    int rc = app.exec();
    responder.stop();
.EE
Run the demo and scan it from Python:
.EX
    cmake -S cpp -B cpp/build && cmake --build cpp/build
    ./cpp/build/responder_demo --app demo --port 5557 --seconds 30 &
    mu2edaq-discover --filter app=demo
.EE
.SH NOTES
Start the responder only after the service's own socket is bound, so discovery
never advertises a port that is not accepting connections.
.PP
The C++ responder does not enforce
.B MAX_DATAGRAM
on the ANNOUNCE it builds, and it does not reject a DISCOVER whose
.B filter
is not an object, whose filter values are not strings, or that names a filter
key outside
.BR app / name / host .
The Python responder rejects all of those and stays silent.
The two therefore answer a different set of malformed queries.
.PP
The
.B build_announce
field order differs from Python's for the
.B qid
key: C++ appends it after
.BR started ,
Python inserts it after
.BR pid .
JSON object order is not significant, so this is cosmetic.
